## TL;DR — Asscher vs Cushion Cut Diamond

**Core finding:** These two shapes are not comparable on sparkle. Cushion is a brilliant cut — it produces fire, scintillation, and crushed-ice sparkle. [Asscher cut diamonds](/asscher-cut-diamond) are step cuts — they produce long mirrored corridors and zero brilliant sparkle.

If you want square-ish diamonds with sparkle, buy cushion. If you want the octagonal windmill step-cut look, buy asscher.

**Price:** Cushion costs less than asscher at every size in both natural and lab inventory. At 1ct, cushion G-VS1 is $573 less than asscher E-VS1. Cushion also requires lower clarity grades — SI1 can be eye-clean in cushion; asscher needs VS1 minimum.

**Best natural cushion entry (G-VS1):** → [GIA 1.00ct G-VS1 at $1,891](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/23740863?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational)

**Best natural asscher entry (F-VS1):** → [GIA 1.00ct F-VS1 at $2,521](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/29365844?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational)

**Best lab cushion (E-VVS2):** → [IGI 1.50ct E-VVS2 at $1,422](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/28961887?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational)

**Best lab asscher (D-IF):** → [IGI 1.52ct D-IF at $2,795](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/27991043?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational)

**Priority:** Make the optical decision first. If you want sparkle in a square-ish stone, buy cushion — it costs less, forgives inclusions, and is more widely available. If you specifically want the Art Deco step-cut look with mirrored corridors and geometric depth, buy asscher and budget for VS1 clarity and G minimum color.

---

Asscher vs cushion cut diamond is a question that sounds like a shape comparison but is actually an optical question. These two shapes look nothing alike when worn on a finger in real light.

Cushion cut belongs to the brilliant family. It throws fire, sparkle, and flashes of white and colored light across a room.

Asscher belongs to the step-cut family. It produces deep, geometric mirrored corridors — the same optical family as emerald cut, with no fire and no crushed-ice sparkle.

Choosing between them based on shape alone — "both are squarish" — misses the most important difference. This guide covers the optical difference, the clarity difference, the price difference, and the exact data you need to decide.

---

## The Core Difference: Step Cut vs Brilliant Cut

The single most important thing to understand about asscher vs cushion is that they are in different facet families.

Cushion cut is a brilliant cut. It has 58–64+ facets arranged to scatter and refract light in every direction. The facets are triangular and kite-shaped, pointing inward and outward.

The result is the sparkle that most people picture when they think of diamonds — fire, scintillation, and bright white flashes.

Asscher cut is a step cut. Its facets are flat, parallel, and run in horizontal rows across the stone. Light enters, travels down the corridors, and reflects back in long geometric patterns rather than scattered sparkle.

The result is the "hall of mirrors" or windmill corridor effect that asscher buyers love. It is quiet, architectural, and deeply personal — you either love it or you find it cold.

Neither cut is superior. They are different tools for different buyers with different tastes. But you must know which one you want before you look at price, because no amount of budget will turn an asscher into a cushion or vice versa.

---

## Asscher vs Cushion — Shape and Optical Comparison

This table covers the core differences between the two shapes.

| Feature | Asscher Cut | Cushion Cut | What this means |
|---|---|---|---|
| Facet Family | Step cut — flat parallel facets | Brilliant cut — triangular and kite facets | Completely different optical output from the same carat weight |
| Sparkle Type | Geometric mirrored corridors (windmill) | Fire + scintillation + crushed-ice sparkle | Asscher = no sparkle; cushion = maximum sparkle |
| Shape Outline | Square, octagonal (cropped corners) | Square or slightly rectangular with rounded/pillow edges | Both are "squarish" but look nothing alike face-up |
| 1ct Dimensions | ~5.5 × 5.5mm | ~5.0–5.2mm square | Asscher has slightly more face-up area at 1ct |
| Typical Depth % | 64–68% | 61–67% | Similar depth range — comparable face-up appearance |
| L/W Ratio | 1.00–1.05 (square) | 1.00–1.10 (square) or wider | Both offer square options at similar ratios |
| GIA Cut Grade | Not assigned | Excellent or Ideal available | Cushion gets a quality grade; asscher does not |
| Clarity Minimum | VS1 (step facets amplify inclusions) | SI1–VS2 (brilliant facets mask inclusions) | Cushion lets you buy lower clarity for less money |
| Color Minimum | G (step facets collect color) | H–I acceptable (sparkle masks color) | Cushion lets you go lower on color and save money |
| Inclusion Behavior | Amplified — flat facets expose inclusions | Masked — brilliant facets scatter light | The single biggest practical difference for buyers |
| Setting Metal | White metal needs G minimum | H works in white metal | Cushion is more forgiving on color in any metal |
| Availability | Lower — fewer asscher stones at each size | Higher — large inventory at all sizes | More cushion options means better value selection |

**What this means:** Cushion lets you buy lower clarity and color grades while remaining eye-clean. Asscher requires stricter grades at every tier. The result is that cushion delivers better value per dollar at identical visual quality.

**Verdict:** If you want a squarish diamond with brilliant sparkle, cushion wins on every practical dimension — lower cost, easier clarity, more availability. Asscher wins only on its unique optical character, which no brilliant cut can replicate.

---

## The Brilliance Fork

Every buyer comparing asscher and cushion reaches a fork in the road. Call it the Brilliance Fork — the point where the decision splits based on what kind of light performance you want from your diamond.

On one side of the fork is the cushion cut. It has brilliant facets, fire, and scintillation. The stone throws colored and white flashes of light in every direction as it moves.

In sunlight, it catches your eye from across a table.

On the other side is the asscher cut. It has step facets and long geometric corridors. The stone looks like a pool of still water with concentric square rings reflecting in the depths.

It does not throw sparkle — it draws you inward.

These two optical characters are fundamentally incompatible. There is no hybrid. There is no stone that gives you both the windmill corridors and the brilliant fire.

The Brilliance Fork is also a price fork. Cushion is a more popular shape with higher demand and larger inventory. Paradoxically, it costs less than asscher at most size tiers — more buyers means more supply, which keeps prices competitive.

Asscher is a rarer cut. Fewer rough diamonds have the geometry to yield a clean square step-cut stone. The supply is smaller, which drives prices higher even though the shape is less sought after.

The practical result: choosing cushion over asscher saves you money and gives you more sparkle. Choosing asscher over cushion costs you more money and trades sparkle for an optical character that cushion cannot produce.

There is only one right answer to the Brilliance Fork: know which side you are on before you look at price.

