## The Clarity Buffer

Cushion and asscher require completely different clarity strategies. This difference alone can change your budget by $1,500–$3,000 on a 1ct stone at equal visual quality.

Call it the Clarity Buffer. Cushion's brilliant facets act as a buffer against inclusions. The 58-64+ triangular facets scatter light in every direction, breaking up the field of view so that any inclusion inside the stone is surrounded by competing flashes of light.

The result: SI1 is often eye-clean in a cushion. VS2 is almost always eye-clean. The inclusions exist, but the light performance around them prevents your eye from finding them.

Asscher's flat step facets do the opposite. Instead of scattering light, they act as mirrors. Each flat facet reflects the pavilion, the table, and anything inside the stone directly back to your eye with no interference.

An inclusion that is invisible in a cushion is visible in an asscher. The step-cut corridors amplify inclusions in the same way the Mirror Multiplication Effect reflects internal flaws repeatedly through the octagonal facet rings.

The practical result is a one-tier minimum clarity gap between the two shapes. In cushion, VS2 is your comfortable minimum — VS1 if you want certainty. In asscher, VS1 is the floor with zero exceptions, and VS2 requires individual photo review.

If you buy SI1 in asscher, you will see the inclusion face-up. Every time. This is not a theoretical risk — it is an optical guarantee.

The Clarity Buffer has a direct dollar value. At 1ct, the step up from VS2 to VS1 costs roughly $300–$500 — in asscher you must pay it, in cushion you can often skip it.

Over a 2ct purchase, that difference compounds significantly.

