## Master Decision Matrix: Asscher vs Cushion Cut

This matrix covers the full decision for every buyer type.

| Decision Factor | Choose Asscher | Choose Cushion | Notes |
|---|---|---|---|
| Optical priority | Step-cut corridors, mirrored windmill | Brilliant sparkle, fire, crushed ice | The only decision that matters — all others follow from this |
| Budget | Lower budget: cushion saves $457–$7,500 | ✅ Better value at every size | Cushion saves money at 1ct, 2ct, and 3ct |
| Clarity flexibility | VS1 minimum — no exceptions | SI1–VS2 often eye-clean | Cushion saves $300–$500+ in clarity cost alone |
| Color flexibility | G minimum in white metal | H works in white metal | Cushion saves $200–$400 in color cost |
| Available inventory | Lower — fewer stones | ✅ Higher — larger selection | More cushion options means better value hunting |
| GIA cut grade | Not available for asscher | Excellent / Ideal available | You can't verify cushion cut without checking GIA grade |
| Halo setting | Square halo works well | ✅ Round halo looks exceptional | Cushion's rounded perimeter suits halo better |
| Finger elongation | No elongation — square focal point | No elongation — soft square glow | Neither shape elongates; for elongation choose oval or emerald |
| Vintage / Art Deco | ✅ Strongly associated (1902 origin) | Moderately associated | Asscher has deeper Art Deco history |
| Setting versatility | Solitaire, bezel, step-side three-stone | Solitaire, halo, bezel, three-stone | Cushion is more versatile in setting styles |
| Lab value | More expensive in lab at every size | ✅ Less expensive in lab at every size | Lab cushion is $1,373–$3,843 cheaper at same tiers |
| Who buys this | Step-cut lovers, Art Deco buyers | Everyone else who wants sparkle | Asscher is a specific aesthetic choice; cushion is universal |

**What this means:** Cushion wins on every practical metric — price, clarity tolerance, availability, and cut grading. Asscher wins only on its unique step-cut optical character.

If you are buying asscher, you are buying it because you love what it does optically. That is the only valid reason to pay the premium.

**Verdict:** Cushion is the smarter buy in isolation. Asscher is the right buy if you specifically want the step-cut look and no brilliant cut will satisfy you.

The decision should be made with your eyes — look at photos and videos of both shapes face-up, in motion, in different lighting. If one speaks to you and the other does not, the decision is already made.

---

## Final Verdict: Which Should You Buy?

**Buyer 1 — Budget-conscious, wants maximum sparkle:** Buy cushion without hesitation. At 1ct, [G-VS1 cushion at $1,891](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/23740863?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) delivers brilliant sparkle and an eye-clean stone for $573 less than the entry-level asscher. At 2ct, the saving exceeds $4,800.

The money you save on the stone can go toward the setting, a better carat weight, or simply staying in budget.

**Buyer 2 — Committed to the step-cut look, considering asscher:** Buy asscher and budget for VS1 clarity and G minimum color — do not compromise either. At 1ct, [F-VS1 asscher at $2,521](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/29365844?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) is the correct entry point.

Do not try to save money by dropping to VS2 or SI1 in asscher. The step-cut facets will expose the inclusion and you will see it every day.

**Buyer 3 — Lab buyer choosing between shapes:** Buy cushion if budget is tight. [Lab cushion E-VVS2 1.50ct at $1,422](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/28961887?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) is $1,373 less than [lab asscher D-IF 1.52ct at $2,795](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/27991043?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational).

If the step-cut look is the priority, buy lab asscher — the savings over natural are significant and the quality at D-IF is extraordinary.

**Buyer 4 — Undecided, looking at both shapes side by side:** Stop looking at prices and look at face-up photos of both shapes in motion. Watch how they react to light.

Asscher throws geometric flashes of mirrored light in quiet waves. Cushion throws brilliant fire in every direction.

Most buyers know within sixty seconds which one speaks to them. If you are genuinely undecided after seeing both in motion, cushion is the practical choice — more sparkle, more availability, lower cost.

> **Farzana says:** I've sold both for years. Cushion buyers walk in wanting sparkle — they want the ring to catch light across the room. Asscher buyers walk in wanting something different, something quieter, something that rewards people who look closely. They're not making a better choice or a worse choice. They're making a different choice. The mistake is choosing asscher because you think it's "more refined" without knowing what step-cut optics actually feel like to wear. Always see both shapes face-up before you commit. Always.

---

## Expert Summary

Asscher and cushion cut diamonds share a square-ish shape outline but produce completely different optical experiences. Cushion is a brilliant cut — it sparkles, throws fire, and masks inclusions in its 58–64 facets. Asscher is a step cut — it creates mirrored octagonal corridors, has no sparkle, and amplifies inclusions in its flat parallel facets.

The practical consequences of this difference are significant. Cushion buyers can buy SI1–VS2 clarity and H color in white metal and remain eye-clean. Asscher buyers must buy VS1 clarity and G minimum color — no exceptions.

The price difference compounds these savings. At 1ct, [cushion G-VS1 at $1,891](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/23740863?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) costs $573 less than [asscher E-VS1 at $2,464](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/28919719?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational). At 2ct, the gap reaches $4,854–$7,534.

In lab, cushion is cheaper at every tier — [lab cushion E-VVS2 1.50ct at $1,422](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/28961887?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) vs [lab asscher D-IF 1.52ct at $2,795](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/27991043?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational).

The decision comes down to optics. If you want brilliant sparkle in a squarish diamond, buy cushion — it is the better value by every measure.

If you want the Art Deco step-cut hall-of-mirrors look with no brilliant competition, buy asscher and budget correctly. For complete asscher guidance on clarity and color, see the [asscher cut diamond clarity guide](/asscher-cut-diamond-clarity) and [asscher cut diamond color guide](/asscher-cut-diamond-color-guide).

