## Frequently Asked Questions

### What is the difference between asscher cut and cushion cut diamonds?

Asscher cut is a step cut — flat parallel facets that create geometric mirrored corridors. Cushion cut is a brilliant cut — triangular facets that produce fire, scintillation, and sparkle.

Both shapes are roughly square, but they look completely different face-up. Asscher is quiet and architectural. Cushion sparkles in every direction.

### Which has more sparkle, asscher or cushion cut?

Cushion has significantly more sparkle. It is a brilliant cut with 58–64+ facets designed to maximize light return, fire, and scintillation.

Asscher has no sparkle in the brilliant sense. It produces geometric flashes of reflected light in long mirrored corridors. If you want a sparkling square diamond, cushion is the only choice.

### Is asscher or cushion cut more expensive?

Asscher is more expensive than cushion at every size tier in both natural and lab-grown inventory. At 1ct, asscher E-VS1 costs [$2,464](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/28919719?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) vs cushion G-VS1 at [$1,891](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/23740863?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) — a $573 difference.

At 2ct, the gap reaches $4,854–$7,534. In lab-grown, cushion is $1,373–$3,843 cheaper at comparable size tiers.

### What clarity grade do I need for cushion cut vs asscher cut?

Cushion: SI1 to VS2 is often eye-clean. VS1 gives you certainty. The brilliant facets scatter light and conceal inclusions effectively.

Asscher: VS1 is the minimum with no exceptions. VS2 requires individual stone photo review. SI1 will show inclusions face-up — always.

### What color grade do I need for cushion vs asscher?

Asscher needs G minimum in white gold and platinum. The step facets collect body color and reflect it through the corridors, making H visibly warm in white metal.

Cushion is more forgiving — H works well in white gold and platinum for most buyers. I can work in yellow or rose gold.

### Which is better for an engagement ring, asscher or cushion?

Neither is universally better — it depends on the buyer's optical preference. Cushion is better if you want sparkle, more setting versatility, lower cost, and easier clarity requirements.

Asscher is better if you want the Art Deco step-cut look with mirrored corridors and geometric depth. There is no way to get both the windmill corridors and brilliant sparkle in the same stone.

### Does asscher cut look bigger than cushion cut?

Asscher has slightly more face-up area at the same carat weight. A 1ct asscher is approximately 5.5×5.5mm, while a 1ct cushion is typically 5.0–5.2mm square.

However, cushion's rounded edges and brilliant sparkle create a visual presence that many buyers perceive as equally large. The difference is small at 1ct and less relevant than the optical character difference.

### Can cushion cut pass as asscher cut?

No. They produce completely different optical effects. Asscher's windmill corridor pattern — the concentric octagonal rings when viewed face-up — is unique to step-cut squares.

Cushion's brilliant sparkle cannot be replicated by any step cut. If you look at a cushion and an asscher side by side, there is zero confusion about which is which.

### Which is better for hiding inclusions, asscher or cushion?

Cushion is significantly better at hiding inclusions. The brilliant facets scatter light and prevent inclusions from drawing the eye. SI1 is often eye-clean in cushion.

Asscher is the worst shape for hiding inclusions. The flat step facets act as mirrors, amplifying inclusions and making them visible at grades that would be invisible in a cushion.

### Which shape holds its value better, asscher or cushion?

Neither shape holds resale value well — all diamonds depreciate significantly from retail. Cushion has higher secondary market liquidity due to higher demand and more buyers.

Asscher is a rarer taste and resells slower, though the per-carat price point is higher. For investment purposes, neither shape competes with round brilliant.

### Is cushion cut a good alternative to asscher cut?

Cushion is not an alternative to asscher — they are fundamentally different shapes producing different optical experiences. Cushion is an alternative to round brilliant (same brilliant family, different shape). Asscher is an alternative to emerald cut (same step-cut family, different outline).

If you want step-cut corridors and are flexible on square vs rectangle, asscher and emerald are alternatives to each other. If you want brilliant sparkle in a square stone, cushion and princess are alternatives.

### Which is rarer, asscher or cushion cut?

Asscher is significantly rarer. The square step-cut geometry requires specific rough diamond shapes, and the cut is less popular than cushion globally.

Cushion cut is one of the most popular diamond shapes in the world. It has a large inventory at every size tier and is actively produced by most major cutting houses.

### What are the ideal proportions for asscher vs cushion cut?

Asscher ideal proportions: depth 62–68%, table 60–68%, L/W ratio 1.00–1.05, no culet, thin to medium girdle. GIA does not assign a cut grade for asscher — check these specs manually on the certificate.

Cushion ideal proportions: depth 61–67%, table 58–68%, L/W ratio 1.00–1.10 for square. GIA assigns Excellent or Ideal cut grades for cushion — always buy Excellent or Ideal.

